---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

FIESTA.m prepares a key-value database for integration with the use of KLink (kyotocabinet).
The structure of the database is the following:

key: 0-
value: list of tasks (for example, {1,2})
in standart SDEvaluate databases it will be usually equal to {1} (one task)
each task will be further denoted with <task>

key: 0-RegVar
value: name of the additional regularization variable; can be missing

key: 0-SmallVar
value: name of the additional variable tending to zero; only in SDExpandAsy

key: 0-RequestedOrders
value: pair of requested orders for ep and small var; only in SDExpandAsy

key: <task>-UsingC
value: True or False; a prepared database can be used only with the same UsingC setting

key: <task>-ExternalExponent
value: the exponent of small variable; everything is multiplied by t^value; it is 0 for basic examples

key: <task>-Exact
value: True or False; indicates whether we know an excat answer

in case of True for <task>-Exact we have only one more key for this <task>:

key: <task>-ExactValue
value: result for this <task>

otherwise we have the following keys:

key: <task>-ForEvaluation
value: list of pairs {ep order, {extvar order, extvar log order}} - coefficients that have to be evaluated

key: <task>-ForEvaluationString
value: same but elements are separated with | and ep order and extvar pair with -
example: 1-{1,1}|
the terms separated will be called <intdeg> below

key: <task>-SCounter
value: number of sectors; each <intdeg> will have SCounter integration terms (some are equal to zero)

key: <task>-<intdeg>-T
value: number of non-zero terms corresponding to <intdeg>; only for output

key: <task>-<intdeg>-N
value: max number of variables in integrals with <intdeg>

key: <task>-<intdeg>-<num>
value: number of subterms in sector <num> for order <intdeg>

key: <task>-<intdeg>-<num>-<num2>
value: the integration term num2 in sector num for order <intdeg>
the term is ended with ;\\012

key: <task>-runorder
value: maximal order to run integration till; coincides with the maximal value of first parts of <intdeg>

---------------
the following terms are needed only for final result collection
---------------

key: <task>-SHIFT
value: order shift between requested order and runorder; corresponds to the ep-order of external terms

key: <task>-EXTERNAL
value: external terms outside of integration

key: <task>-EXTERNAL-<deg>
value: numerical series coefficient of EXTERNAL of order <deg>

key: <task>-EXTERNAL-<deg>E
value: exact series coefficient of EXTERNAL of order <deg>

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The binaries CIntegratePool and CIntegratePoolMPI use this database for integration. They have the following options:

-in <path> - path to input database
-out <path> - path to output database; used only in -direct mode
-bucket <num> - database setting; for details refer to kyotocabinet documentation
-integrator <string> - integration choice, by default: vegasCuba
-intpar <key> <vaue> - integrator option
-math <path> - sets the path to Mathematica binary for CIntegrateMP (see below)

The following options are related to multiple precision integration tuning
-MPThreshold <num> 
-MPPrecision <num>
-PrecisionShift <num>
-SmallX <num>
-MPMin <num>

-threads <num> - number of threads; ignored my CIntegratePoolMPI
-all - integrate with all <task>
-task <task> integrate with chosen <task>
-prefix <intdeg> - integrate only chosen <intdeg>
-test - only test integration, no database usage
-direct - called alone, not from Mathematica; results in writing in output database and writing to stdout; with no -direct writes results to files
-CIntegratePath <path> - path to CIntegrateMP or CIntegrateMPC
-notest - skip the integration consistency test (good for special integration options of justEvaluate integrator)
-CubaCores <num> - internal parralization option for the Cuba integrator

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The binaries CIntegratePool and CIntegratePoolMPI (or Mathematica at the SDIntegrate[] stage) fill the second database with results.

The second database has the following entries:

0-, 0-RegVar, 0-SmallVar,

<task>-Exact

and depending on its value

<task>-ExactValue

or remaining keys

<task>-ForEvaluation, <task>-ExpandVariable, <task>-runorder, <task>-SHIFT, <task>-EXTERNAL, <task>-EXTERNAL-<order>
their values are just copied from the first database.

key: <task>-<prefix>-R
value: result for this task and prefix

key: <task>-<prefix>-E
value: False or True, indicates whether the result was calculated exactly. If it was perfomed by CIntegratePool, it is always False

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The binaries CIntegrateMP and CIntegrateMPC should normally not be called directly, however you can use them to test integration or to
use it from your separate programs.

After being launched the binary waits for input. The input command should be ended with new line. Most commands require an argument ended with new line
(setting integration parameters requires two arguments). The command result is printed back. The commands are the following:

exit, Exit, quit or Quit - exit immidiately
help - list commands

MPFR, Native or Mixed (default) sets the evaluation mode

Debug - turns all printing of all results
TestF - tests the F function for being positive instead of integrating
Statistics - prints timings

SetIntegrator - set integrator equal to the next argument
SetCurrentIntegratorParameter - set integrator parameter (two arguments)
GetCurrentIntegratorParameters - print out actual parameters

CPUCores - sets the number of CPU calculating cores = the cubacores setting of Cuba
GPUCores - sets the number of GPU calculating cores = the cubaaccel setting of Cuba
GPUForceCore - specifies the GPU core that will be used (in case of GPUCores=1)
GPUMemoryPart - limits the GPU memory that will be used by the process (default = 1 = full)

The following options are related to multiple precision integration tuning
SetMPPrecision (integer argument)
SetMPPrecisionShift (integer argument)
SetSmallX (float argument %f)
SetMPThreshold (float argument %f)
SetMPMin (float argument %f)

Integrate - the main integration command. The integrand format should be the following:
<nvars>;
<terms>;
<terms> - lines with terms ending with ;
<final expression>;

<terms> can be 0, in this case the final expression comes right after the number of vars
the terms or the final expression can refer preveous terms with f[n] (n starts from 1)
integration variables are refered with x[n] (n starts from 1)
power goes as p[_,_], logarithm as l[_], Pi as P, EulerGamma as G, PolyGamma is not shortened.

Parse - same, but only check the input, do not integrate. Good answer is {0,0}

SetMath - provides path to the Mathematica binary. If it is provided, CIntegrateMP can call Mathematica and evaluate some constants
          currently the only constant working this way is PolyGamma[_,_]
          some of the values of PolyGamma are stored in constants.h
          setting math is not recommended if you are using massive thread or mpi calculations (each slave will call math!)
          a better approach to be safe is to use OnlyPrepare in Mathematica, then run your job
          if some constants are missing you can add them to constants.h, recompile the binaries and launch the job again
          and since this version of FIESTA makes a complete parse check of all expressions before integrating, it won't take much time.

